## 16. Risks and mitigations

| Risk | Impact | Mitigation | Owner/status |
|---|---|---|---|
| Red browser suite | Broken journeys or stale expectations can ship unnoticed | Classify all 52 failures, fix defects first, approve intentional visual change manually, rerun full suite | `[TBD]`; open blocker |
| Historical evidence applied to new code | False readiness claim | Require exact-commit evidence after every relevant change | Engineering/release; required |
| Duplicate transfer after lost response | Double movement | Preserve intent/idempotency key, warn users, expose safe identical retry | Implemented; reverify |
| Stale cache represented as current | Incorrect user decision | Versioned consistency requirement, bounded wait, PostgreSQL fallback, truthful failure | Implemented; reverify |
| Cross-tenant disclosure | Financial/privacy breach | Server-side tenant/subject/object authorization and non-disclosing errors | Implemented; managed security proof pending |
| Funding language implies settlement | Regulatory/user misunderstanding | State that funding records external confirmation and credit occurs only after balanced posting | Implemented copy requires named approval |
| Delivery state confused with posting | Unsafe retries and support errors | Display financial and delivery states separately | Implemented; UI requalification pending |
| WebRTC data leakage | Sensitive evidence exposure | Two participants, authorization on every request, ephemeral text, no transcript/logging, bounded envelopes, TURN gate | Implemented locally; production gate open |
| Shared limiter/signalling unavailable | Abuse exposure or misleading failure | Fail closed for protected capability while leaving financial state unchanged | Implemented configuration contract; managed proof pending |
| Migration collision/history drift | Deployment failure | Forward compatibility migration, ordered migration tests, no history rewrite | Implemented; exact release rehearsal required |
| Inadequate physical accessibility evidence | Operators cannot complete critical work | Named device and assistive-technology review before shared pilot | External gate |
| Missing legal/finance decisions | Unauthorized product operation | Obtain signed jurisdiction, currency, custody wording, retention, limits, and pause authority | External gate |
| Managed restore unproven | Permanent loss or unacceptable recovery | Provider PITR drill, cache rebuild, reconciliation, signed RPO/RTO | External gate |

## 17. Dependencies and assumptions

- Initial financial movement remains internal and same-currency.
- Account identity and ownership data exist before a transfer is enabled.
- The first users are authenticated operators and a small explicitly authorized operations group.
- PostgreSQL remains the source of financial truth.
- Redis may be lost and rebuilt without changing financial truth.
- Corrections are compensating entries, not mutations of history.
- Production identity, cloud account, legal approval, named owners, budget, and partners are not currently supplied.
- The current local feature flag does not authorize production WebRTC use.
- Any conflict between this PRD and an approved finance/security/legal decision must be resolved through a versioned PRD update before release.

## 18. Recommended next steps

1. Assign a named release owner and freeze one candidate commit for remediation.
2. Triage the 52 Playwright failures into product defects, stale fixtures, and intentional visual changes; record one owner and exit test for every failure.
3. Fix account command, onboarding/orientation, reconciliation, investigation-search, session, and pagination failures before touching visual baselines.
4. Review each visual difference manually and update only approved baselines.
5. Rerun frontend lint, TypeScript, unit/security/UI, full Playwright, visual, performance, and production build gates.
6. Rerun complete Go, migration, PostgreSQL/Redis integration, race, fault, recovery, and container gates.
7. Repeat the real-stack core financial journey and two-operator investigation journey against disposable data.
8. Publish exact-commit evidence and update the master delivery register; do not reuse historical pass claims.
9. Conduct named physical-device, keyboard, screen-reader, and moderated operator reviews.
10. Decide whether the release target is only the local MVP or a managed design-partner pilot; if it is a pilot, assign owners for every external gate before provisioning anything.

## 19. Additional beneficial enhancements

Only already-documented enhancements are included here:

- Complete the API-first developer milestone: recipes, bulk provisioning, shared replay protection, endpoint verification, and exact-commit evidence.
- Complete the operator IA and shared design-system modularization already tracked in the master register.
- Add the separately planned public website only after legal, privacy, accessibility, abuse, and evidence requirements are approved.
- Implement managed identity, observability, backup/PITR, and incident operations before shared use.
- Evaluate the separately specified provider-led INR payout domain only after provider, legal, finance, security, and operations gates pass.

These enhancements are not approved for production merely by appearing in this document.

